¶
'''veraciously''' (vẹ̄-rā′shus-li), ''adv.'' In a veracious manner; truthfully.

'''veracity''' (vẹ̄-ras′i̤-ti), ''n.'' [< OF. ''veracitie'', F. ''véracité'' = Sp. ''veracidad'' = Pg. ''veracidade'' = It. ''veracità'', < ML. ''veracita(t-)s'', truthfulness, < L. ''verax'' (''verac-''), truthful: see ''veracious''.] '''1.''' The fact or character of being veracious or true. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') Habitual regard to or observance of truth; truthfulness; truth: as, a man of ''veracity''.}}
{{smaller block|Let ''veracity'' be thy virtue, in words, manners, and actions.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Christ. Mor., iii. 20.}}
Another form of virtue which usually increases with civilisation is ''veracity'', a term which must be regarded as including something more than the simple avoidance of direct falsehood.
{{right|''Lecky'', Europ. Morals, I. 143.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Consistency with truth; agreement with actual fact: as, the ''veracity'' of the senses.}}
{{smaller block|In narratives, where historical ''veracity'' has no place, I cannot discover why there should not be exhibited the most perfect idea of virtue.
{{right|''Johnson'', Rambler, No. 4.}}
That enthusiasm for truth, that fanaticism of ''veracity'', which is a greater possession than much learning; a nobler gift than the power of increasing knowledge.
{{right|''Huxley'', Universities.}}}}
'''2.''' That which is true; that in which truth inheres; also, abstract truth.—'''Principle of veracity.''' {{smaller|(''a'') The proposition that man has a natural inclination or propensity toward speaking the truth. (''b'') The proposition that God’s veracity requires us to accept without doubt a given wide-spread belief. This was urged by the English Platonists and others. (''c'') The proposition that innate beliefs must be accepted on account of the veracity of consciousness.}}—'''Veracity of consciousness,''' {{smaller|the conformity of natural beliefs to the truth.}}

